f data points: \\(5, 10, 15, 20\\).\n\nFirst, calculate the sum of the data points:\n\n\\[\n5 + 10 + 15 + 20 = 50\n\\]\n\nNext, divide the sum by the number of data points (which is 4):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50}{4} = 12.5\n\\]\n\nNow, if you add zero to any of the data points, the sum of the data points will remain the same, and the mean will not change. For example, if you add 0 to the first data point:\n\n\\[\n(5 + 0) + 10 + 15 + 20 = 50\n\\]\n\nDivide the new sum by the number of data points:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50}{4} = 12.5\n\\]\n\nIn this scenario, adding zero to any data point does not change the mean. This is a practical application of the zero property of addition in statistical calculations.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12.5}\n\\]\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. <|User|>Thank you for the statistical example. It really helps to see how the zero property of addition is used in practical statistical situations.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the statistical example helpful. The zero property of addition is indeed crucial in statistical contexts, ensuring that small adjustments or corrections do not affect the overall mean.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. <|User|>Could you provide an example of a problem where the zero property of addition is crucial in a physics context?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The zero property of addition is crucial in physics, particularly in calculations involving vectors and physical quantities. Let's consider a real-world physics example:\n\n**Example: Vector Addition**\n\nIn physics, vectors are quantities that have both magnitude and direction. Suppose you have two vectors: \\(\\vec{A} = 5 \\hat{i}\\) and \\(\\vec{B} = 10 \\hat{i}\\).\n\nFirst, add the vectors:\n\n\\[\n\\vec{A} + \\vec{B} = 5 \\hat{i} + 10 \\hat{i} = 15 \\hat{i}\n\\]\n\nNow, if you add a zero v